  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rounds of showers through Monday. Rain may be \nheavy at times Sunday, especially across Downeast Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure off of Cape Cod will move northeast this morning, \npassing just south of the Gulf of Maine. Given the track of the \nlow think the best chance for pops will be north and east of the\nlow center this morning with the bulk of the rain occurring \nnear the New Brunswick border. PW values in far southeastern \nareas rising to close to 99th percentile with amounts between \n1.50-2.00 inches. Any rainfall will be helpful to ongoing \ndrought conditions given that we will be in a cloudy cool \npattern for the next couple days. This will allow for rainfall \nto get into the soils, unlikely to help streamflows which are \nsignificantly below normal.\n\nWeak low pressure will still be back over the western Gulf of \nMaine and becoming absorbed by the occlusion over Quebec. \nAlthough pwats remain high over the southern and eastern half of\nthe area have reduced pops in the afternoon with parts of the \narea lacking forcing and the low pressure over the gulf weaker \nand further to the east. Given the 160- 180% of normal PWATs \n(95-99th percentile) showers may produce heavy rainfall at \ntimes. Showers will be more convective during the day so \nuncertain where the beneficial rainfall will occur. Then \nthroughout Sunday night, the low becomes stationary over Quebec \nas it rotates the moisture through the state. By Monday, the low\nbegins to gradually move north which will decrease rain chances\nthroughout the day. However, best chance of showers and \nthunderstorms will be in the northern 1/2 of the CWA closer to \nthe vertically stacked low in Quebec. QPF totals through Monday \nafternoon are mainly less than 0.25 inch, with up to 0.75 inch \nin Washington county. Isolated spots up to 0.5 inch possible in\nstronger/training showers. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper level low pressure keeps the chance of \nshowers going into midweek, especially across Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure stalls over Canada, slowly meandering to the east \nthis week. Looking at chances of rain showers in the north, on \nTuesday and Wednesday. There is some instability during the \nafternoons, with some models hinting at 500-1,000 J/kg of CAPE, \n7C/km low level lapse rates, and 30-40 kts of effective shear. \nPWAT values roughly 1.00-1.25\", so rain showers expected to be \nmostly light, with higher totals possible in any convection.\nThat being said, not anticipating severe weather. Temperatures \ndrop Tuesday into the upper-60s to low-70s, and raise back to \nthe 70s on Wednesday.",
